  Dead Space openly communicates to you  how you are going to get scared  Necromorphs always come out of vents  unless they're already mulling around in  the areas you have stepped into you can  chart precisely where they will appear  sometimes even seeing them drop down or  Scuttle along the ventilation of the  ishimura before popping out with the  musical's thing to give you a big hug  the ventilation shafts also burst wide  open when they jump out so you have a  visual marker of where they are coming  from one of the greatest examples of  this communication is during the lead-up  to the Leviathan encounter after leaving  the greenhouses in Hydroponics you'll  enter a door towards food storage and  this happens  At first I felt confused and a little  cheated by this otherwise unexpected  scare but then I realized  I knew this was coming there were these  massive holes ripped out of the ishimura  leading up to Hydroponics which I  assumed to be more spaces for the  regular Necromorphs more specifically  those tentacle-headed ones to crawl out  of I mean that's weird but I thought it  was just more of that environmental  storytelling what I realized is that  wasn't this a scene to get my  imagination going I was being warned  and this comes to the other way that  Dead Space expertly crafts its scares  [Music]  in Dead Space you do a lot of  backtracking in pure metroidvania style  you revisit spaces with new knowledge of  which doors are unlocked and new  abilities to use to Traverse the  ishimura walking back over a corpse I  thought I had chopped up only to hear  the musical sting and watch it rise up  behind me was horrifying and  simultaneously brilliant a jump scare  that changes how you think about your  environment subverts your expectations  and adds to the terror of the game is a  well done jump scare to me also it had  me checking every dead Necromorph just  to be sure that they weren't playing  dead  speaking of subverting your expectations  let's talk about elevators aside from a  close call at the beginning of the game  elevators are a safe space even safer  than most safe rooms in game that is  until you get to the mining decks I  didn't think anything of it I thought it  was just another loading elevator you  know maybe some Whispers or scuttling  around in the vents like normal all of a  sudden in Full Metal Gear Solid fashion  I was surrounded by a group of  Necromorphs after that I was sure not to  let my guard down on any elevator ride  this subversion of the world's rules  allows horror and Terror to actively  feed each other instead of creating a  pipeline where horror can only ever  react to Terror and all this is  bolstered by the design elements of the  original Dead Space the necromorph's OG  design as well as every iteration that  comes up are incredibly well crafted  sound design is also extremely important  the musical sting I keep mentioning sets  your nerves on edge and the combinations  of baby cries animal noises and guttural  spewing I'll make the beasts calls and  screams a horrific encounter in Dead  Space in order to progress through the  story you have to engage with the horror  it has to offer  but here's the thing horror is fleeting  I love this definition by Gothic horror  writer Anne Radcliffe when talking about  the difference between Terror and horror  horror contracts freezes and nearly  annihilates the soul after about chapter  six or seven my whole Dead Space  experience became an exhausting slog  through rooms with the same jump scare  setup the scares were never cheap they  were just expected this pattern of the  same scare room to room really started  to wear me down but what kept me playing  and kept me utterly terrified  were the environments I found myself  more terrified than horrified as the  game went on gurgling walls of alien  biology the greenhouses with the canned  Forest noise the super tight  whisper-filled hallways of the executive  levels these all kept me on edge 